x86_64-windows  the closure types, and nothing else: cxx1 names one $_0 where clang-msvc names it <lambda_0>, and clang encodes a deduced return as @ where cxx1 writes the type. [expr.prim.lambda] calls a closure a unique unnamed type and obliges nobody to spell it any particular way; nothing links against one, a call operator being reached through its object. The same difference lambda.nonames and lookup-order.nonames record. x86_64-linux agrees on all 37 names and arm64-darwin agrees too - what this case is about, the eight pieces of per-function state a nested parse has to put back, is checked by run.sh on every box and by the ledger line in its .expected.
